General elections were held in [[Republic of Serbia (1990–2006)|Serbia]] on 20 December 1992. The vote was held only two years after the [[1990 Serbian general election|previous election]] and as a result of [[1992 Serbian early elections referendum|a referendum]] which approved holding early elections.<ref>[http://www.sudd.ch/event.php?lang=en&id=rs041992 Serbien (Jugoslawien), 11. Oktober 1992 : Vorzeitige Neuwahlen des Parlaments] Direct Democracy</ref>

The elections were won by [[Slobodan Milošević]] and his [[Socialist Party of Serbia]] (SPS), which won 101 of the 250 seats in the [[National Assembly of Serbia|National Assembly]]. The SPS formed a government with [[Vojislav Šešelj]]'s [[Serbian Radical Party]]. The presidential election was marked by a total domination of state-run media by Milošević with accusations of [[voter fraud]] and tampering. [[Milan Panić]] refused to accept the result, and stated that he believed he was the true election winner.

== Background ==
[[Slobodan Milošević]] was a candidate of the [[Socialist Party of Serbia]] and was solely endorsed by the [[Serbian Radical Party]]. 	

[[Milan Panić]] was an independent candidate endorsed by the [[Democratic Movement of Serbia]] and the [[Democratic Party (Serbia)|Democratic Party]]. The elections were [[Election boycott|boycotted]] by political parties of ethnic [[Kosovo Albanians]], who made up around 17% of the population.<ref name=CeSID1>{{Cite book|url=http://www.cesid.rs/pdfovi/OKO%20IZBORA%201.pdf |title=Oko izbora 1 |publisher=CeSID |year=1997 }}</ref>

